{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Electromagnetic Induction","count":13,"questions":[{"id":52111,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Electromagnetic Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A conducting bar of mass $\\mathrm{m}$ and length $\\ell$ moves on two frictionless parallel rails in the presence of a constant uniform magnetic field of magnitude B directed into the page as shown in the figure . The bar is given an initial velocity $\\mathrm{v}_{0}$ towards the right at $\\mathrm{t}=0$. Then the\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FYHKeOv97Wt7P9SSFdA5lOCor19OfDIrlwk9Z-dIDWo8.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FYHKeOv97Wt7P9SSFdA5lOCor19OfDIrlwk9Z-dIDWo8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FYHKeOv97Wt7P9SSFdA5lOCor19OfDIrlwk9Z-dIDWo8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["Induced current in the circuit is in the clockwise direction","Velocity of the bar decreases linearly with time","Distance the bar travels before it comes to a complete stop is proportional to $\\mathrm{R}$","Power generated across the resistance is proportional to $\\ell$."],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{F}=\\mathrm{iB} \\ell\\mathrm{a}=\\frac{\\mathrm{iB} \\ell}{\\mathrm{m}}\\phi=\\mathrm{B} . \\mathrm{A}\\frac{\\mathrm{d} \\phi}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\mathrm{B} \\cdot \\ell \\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{dx}}{\\mathrm{dt}}\\right)\\varepsilon=(\\mathrm{B} \\cdot \\ell \\mathrm{v})\\mathrm{i}=\\varepsilon / \\mathrm{R}=\\frac{\\mathrm{B} \\cdot \\ell \\mathrm{v}}{\\mathrm{R}}\\mathrm{a}=\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{B} \\ell \\mathrm{v}}{\\mathrm{R}}\\right) \\frac{\\mathrm{B} \\ell}{\\mathrm{m}}\\mathrm{a}=\\frac{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\ell^{2}}{\\mathrm{Rm}} \\cdot \\mathrm{v}\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{a}=\\mathrm{v} \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{dv}}{\\mathrm{dx}}\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{v} . \\frac{\\mathrm{dv}}{\\mathrm{dx}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\ell^{2}}{\\mathrm{Rm}} \\cdot \\mathrm{v}\\Rightarrow \\int \\mathrm{dv}=\\frac{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\ell^{2}}{\\mathrm{Rm}} \\cdot \\int \\mathrm{d} \\mathrm{x}\\mathrm{v}=\\frac{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\ell^{2}}{\\mathrm{Rm}} \\cdot{ }^{\\prime} \\mathrm{X}^{\\prime}\\left[\\mathrm{X}=\\frac{\\mathrm{vRM}}{\\mathrm{B}^{\\prime} \\ell^{2}}\\right]$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2138403543d6a42b7ac"},{"id":52112,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Electromagnetic Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A square-shaped wire loop of mass $\\mathrm{m}$, resistance $R$ and side 'a' moving with speed $v_{0}$, parallel to the $\\mathrm{x}$-axis, enters a region of uniform magnetic field $\\mathrm{B}$, which is perpendicular to the plane of the loop. The speed of the loop changes with distance $x(x \\lt  a)$ in the filed, as","options":["$v_{0}-\\frac{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\mathrm{a}^{2}}{\\mathrm{Rm}} \\mathrm{x}$","$v_{0}-\\frac{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\mathrm{a}^{2}}{2 \\mathrm{Rm}} \\mathrm{x}$","$v_{0}-\\frac{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\mathrm{a}}{\\mathrm{Rm}} \\mathrm{x}^{2}$","$\\mathrm{v}_{0}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FeAdIO48hbQComl25L-_c22_NYn0aEeFEK2ms1ZMoUjE.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FeAdIO48hbQComl25L-_c22_NYn0aEeFEK2ms1ZMoUjE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FeAdIO48hbQComl25L-_c22_NYn0aEeFEK2ms1ZMoUjE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FhADuGPlh2eUFmDbmG9mCaGhH52xZK0njh75cE6n2cgI.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FhADuGPlh2eUFmDbmG9mCaGhH52xZK0njh75cE6n2cgI.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FhADuGPlh2eUFmDbmG9mCaGhH52xZK0njh75cE6n2cgI.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\mathrm{F}=\\mathrm{i} \\ell \\mathrm{B}=\\frac{\\mathrm{vBa} \\cdot \\mathrm{Ba}}{\\mathrm{R}}\\mathrm{ma}=\\frac{\\mathrm{vB}^{2} \\mathrm{a}^{2}}{\\mathrm{R}}\\frac{-\\mathrm{mdv}}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{vB}^{2} \\mathrm{a}^{2}}{\\mathrm{R}}\\Rightarrow-\\mathrm{m} \\frac{\\mathrm{vdv}}{\\mathrm{dx}}=\\mathrm{v} \\frac{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\mathrm{a}^{2}}{\\mathrm{R}}-\\mathrm{m} \\int_{\\mathrm{v}_{0}} \\mathrm{dv}=\\frac{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\mathrm{a}^{2}}{\\mathrm{R}} \\int_{0}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{d} \\mathrm{x}-\\mathrm{m}\\left(\\mathrm{v}-\\mathrm{v}_{0}\\right)=\\frac{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\mathrm{a}^{2} \\mathrm{x}}{\\mathrm{R}}\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{v}=\\mathrm{v}_{0}-\\frac{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\mathrm{a}^{2}}{\\mathrm{mR}} \\mathrm{x}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213cad55ed88b787db6"},{"id":52113,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Electromagnetic Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>A conducting rod, with a resistor of resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo></math> is pulled with constant speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math> on a smooth conducting rail as shown in figure. A constant magnetic field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>→</mo></mover></math> is directed into the page. If the speed of the bar is doubled, by what factor does the rate of heat dissipation across the resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> change?</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F183e061c-ea73-4edb-8c0f-3feee6ce07e5-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F183e061c-ea73-4edb-8c0f-3feee6ce07e5-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F183e061c-ea73-4edb-8c0f-3feee6ce07e5-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 196px;\" /></p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"<blockquote>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></math><br />\nHeat <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo> </mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo> </mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></math><br />\nGiven <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi>V</mi></math><br />\nSo <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mi>H</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p>\n</blockquote>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da757bff248f8a40f8f4d"},{"id":52114,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Electromagnetic Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A girl brings a positively charged rod near a thin neutral stream of water from a tap. She observes that the water stream bends towards her. Instead, if she were to bring a negatively charged rod near to the steam, it will-","options":["Bend in the same direction","Bend in the opposite direction","Not bend at all","Bend in the opposite direction above and below and rod"],"answer":0,"solution":"Due to induction, bend in same direction","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213812ea14782cb798f"},{"id":52115,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Electromagnetic Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The diagram below shows two circle loops of wire (A and B) centred on and perpendicular to the $\\mathrm{x}$-axis, and oriented with their planes parallel to each other. The $y$-axis passes vertically through loop A (dashed line). There is a current $I_{B}$ in loop B as shown. Possible actions which we might perform on loop A are:\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FBPdN_1yKfmOBpo0aUqZdIHaQkGyfEfA1h4BJQ1TeADA.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FBPdN_1yKfmOBpo0aUqZdIHaQkGyfEfA1h4BJQ1TeADA.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FBPdN_1yKfmOBpo0aUqZdIHaQkGyfEfA1h4BJQ1TeADA.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n(i) Move A to the right along $x$ axis closer to $B$\n(ii) Move A to the left along $x$ axis away from B\n(iii) As viewed from above, rotate A clockwise about y axis\n(iv) As viewed from above, rotate A anticlockwise about y axis\nWhich of these actions will induce a current in A only in the direction shown.","options":["Only (i)","Only (ii)","Only (i) and (iv)","Only (ii) and (iii)"],"answer":0,"solution":"According to Lenz's Law","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213de35aab11abfd0c0"},{"id":52116,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Electromagnetic Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A short solenoid (length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>l</mi></math> and radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math> turns per unit length) lies well inside and on the axis of a very long, coaxial solenoid (length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math>, radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi></math> turns per unit length, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>></mo><mi>r</mi></math>). Current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math> follows in the short solenoid. Choose the correct statement.","options":["There is uniform magnetic field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>I</mi></math> in the long solenoid.","Mutual inductance of the solenoids is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi></math>.","Flux through outer solenoid due to current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math> in the inner solenoid is proportional to the ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></math>.","Mutual inductance of the solenoids is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>r</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>."],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>We use reciprocatory theorem to calculate mutüal inductance of the system of solenoids. </p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fa8c7dffc-afeb-4e5c-883c-0e635dccd23b-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fa8c7dffc-afeb-4e5c-883c-0e635dccd23b-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fa8c7dffc-afeb-4e5c-883c-0e635dccd23b-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>Let outer solenoid carries a current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>. Flux linked with small solenoid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math> is proportional to the current in large solenoid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math>.</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>∝</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⇒</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>∝</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math></p>\n<p>Proportionality constant is the coefficient of mutual inductance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi></math> of system.</p>\n<p>Note that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></math> in all cases.</p>\n<p>So, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>N</mi><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>I</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da71edaa562178db02ee6"},{"id":52117,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Electromagnetic Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Methane is a greenhouse gas because","options":["it absorbs longer wavelengths of the electromagnetic spectrum while transmitting shorter wavelengths","it absorbs shorter wavelengths of the electromagnetic spectrum while transmitting longer wavelengths","it absorbs all wavelengths of the electromagnetic spectrum.","it transmits all wavelengths of the electromagnetic spectrum."],"answer":0,"solution":"No Solution","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213d4426417830095af","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":52118,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Electromagnetic Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A circular loop of 